<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</h1><p class="st-meta">1598 CE – 1846 CE · capital Santa Fe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish province of New Mexico, a remote frontier of New Spain along the upper Rio Grande, scene of the Pueblo Revolt of 1680.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-onate">Juan de Oñate</a> led settlers, soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-real-de-tierra-adentro">Camino Real</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> and claimed the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, setting up headquarters at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewa">Tewa</a> pueblo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohkay-owingeh">Ohkay Owingeh</a>. His brutal punishment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acoma-pueblo">Ácoma Pueblo</a> in 1599 led to his recall. Around 1610 governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-peralta">Pedro de Peralta</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-new-mexico">Santa Fe</a> as the new capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governors ruled from the Palace of the Governors, while Franciscan missions sought to convert the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-peoples">Pueblo peoples</a>, who were burdened with tribute and forced labour under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a>. The province depended on caravans along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-real-de-tierra-adentro">Camino Real</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chihuahua-city">Chihuahua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, and Hispanic villages grew along the river. Raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> shaped frontier life until governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-de-anza">Juan Bautista de Anza</a> made peace with the Comanche in 1786.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope">Popé</a>, drove the Spanish out until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> returned in 1692. After 1821 the province belonged to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>. American troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-w-kearny">Stephen Kearny</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-fe-new-mexico">Santa Fe</a> in 1846, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> ceded it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1848.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico.jpg" alt="Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1598 CE – 1846 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santa Fe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan de Oñate, Diego de Vargas, Juan Bautista de Anza</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Santa Fe de Nuevo México, Nuevo Mexico, New Mexico (Spanish)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1722&amp;focus=santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico" aria-label="Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2004 889 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2324 1145L2321 1717M2324 1145L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2324 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2324 1145h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santa Fe. <a href="/atlas?year=1722&amp;focus=santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

